  {
    "episode_id": 44975,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/4/44975.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 29,
    "turn_text": "Yeah. Well, I think the top takeaways, when you look at the candidates that we were able to be successful with, it was women, it was minorities, and it was veterans. You look at what our communities want and need representation for. And those are many of the areas that I feel like have been typically underrepresented with what's happening in Washington, D.C. And they are truly intersecting with what we're seeing happen in our country right now. I mentioned I'm the mom with the minivan, right? So I'm a per ounce grocery shopper. I go in and when I look at the tags in the store, I'm looking at the cost per ounce. So I know what inflation does to a working family's budget. I know what is going into my grocery cart and what it cost a year ago versus what it costs now. So there's a prime example of how that context coming to the conversation and knowing that I'm representing all of those grocery shop and moms in my community who have the same challenges that our family faces. That's important to add to that conversation. The veterans, for example, who are frustrated because they're having trouble getting care because the personnel records division isn't back to work. And so those veterans are being able to go and advocate in a way because they understand they've been there. They've looked that and they need that experience. Minority communities who have felt like they've been marginalized and their voices haven't been heard. Look at what Hispanic voters are doing in this country because they have felt like they're being minimized in the conversation. And they're increasingly coming out and voting for conservatives because they see Hispanics and minorities representing them in Congress. So I think that that is absolutely critical that Congress should look like people and be able to tell people stories. And that's exactly what I think our freshman class has been able to do very, very successfully in Washington.",
